first lets starts with all the variables:

t: time (speaks for itself)
labda: a variable gor how far u are on the track
x' and y': together they make the point-vector to where the cart is facing
x'' and y'': the variable of how fast x' and y' are changing(the curve in the track)
g: gravity(9.81 here on earth)
v(t): the speed of the cart
a(t): the acceleratiin of the cart:
u(t): help variable, defines as dlabda/dt, how fast labda changes over time t

The big formula:
first left of the triangle we see dlabda^2 / dt^2, which just is how fast the change of labda is(like how a is for speed) 
the right of the triangle:

lets first start with the numerator:
the first part of the numerator is -gy' which you can translate too gravity along the track
you can split this into 2 parts: -g and y'
-g gravity on earth is always pulling down, so the gravity vector is (0, -g)
to know how much gravity is along the track
you take the gravity vector and take the dot product of the gravity vector and the pointvector
which results in a number of how much gravity is pulling in the way to which the pointvector is pointing
the dotproduct gives 0 * x' + -g * y = -gy

the second part of the numerator is (-(x'x''+y'')(dlabda/dt)^2)
you can see this like a math correction factor
this is there because labda is not pre definition constant labda 0 to 1 can cover 1 meter and labda 1 to 2 can cover 10 for example.
It works like this:
We can split this into two parts as well: x'x''y'y'' and (dlabda/dt)^2
x'x'' + y'y'' is the dot product of x'y' and x''y''. If the dotproduct is zero, both of the vectors are perpendicular. 
This means that the track is changing but the "steps" labda takes are constant and there does not have to be a correction factor
If it is not zero it can mean to different things: The labda values can be stretchet or shrunk. 
The dot product measures this and gives how many units dlabda/dt it should change itself so that the model does not break

Then the denumerator: x'^2 + y'^2
This is like the Pythagoras theorem.  
